rs in most countries  including Australia daraprim is sold for  between one and two dollars per pill so  I think that this is a very interesting  story but to be clear they  didn't figure out how to do this that  there's there's a Wikipedia article and  many free resources which explain what  the formula is to synthesize this active  ingredient I don't think that's the  point the headlines were sort of  exaggerated but what is the point pad is  that the price is being charged in the  US are totally immoral price gouging  well wasn't it true that also Martin  shkreli bought the rights to this  particular drug so that no one else was  able to rival him and and compete yeah  oh yeah it was totally a play on being  the only provider and then bring the  price up and shrubs defense at the time  was this is not a drug that a lot of  people use so it's not like we bring up  the price and there might be millions of  people that can't afford this I think he  said there were only about two thousand  people on this drug and that they were  aware of every single one and that even  though they increased the book price  that they were ensuring that no  individuals were unable to get it and if  someone really couldn't afford it they  would give it to them for free that was  his explanation now whether that's true  or not I don't know but what it exposes  this what that explanation exposes that  supply and demand is totally skewed in  health care in the sense that if you're  really sick and you need a drug you  really need that drug and it skews the  entire concept of what a market rate is  and it completely disconnects it from  the costs of making that so here's an  example of sort of the lack of  transparency and this is a very common  situation imagine drug number one costs  $100 is the book price your copay with  insurance is ten bucks sounds pretty  good you just say hey it's ten bucks  that sounds great you have drug number  two it costs $300 at the book rate and  the copay is 25 so you say as a consumer  well I'm gonna go with the ten dollar  drug not the 25 dollar drug except the  manufacturer of the drug is offering a  coupon for patients to cover the twenty  five dollar copay so now patients say  well this drug is ten bucks this other  one is 25 but I've got a coupon for 25  so it's zero I go with the one that's  cheaper for me and it ends up with a  drug that's three times more expensive  being billed to insurance this is  exactly why you  end up with insurance costs going up  premiums going up it's just one aspect  of it just to illustrate one element of  it but this is why this is just  fundamentally not an industry that  should be thought of as an industry  where money is being made and it's why  the free market just shouldn't work in  in the context of health it's not like  you're coming out with a new style of  shoes or new high-tech television or  something this is something that's going  to impact people's health and well-being  and many countries have figured this out  many countries have figured this out  already so in any case headlines  overblown but an interesting story and  it illustrates real issues nonetheless  we will take a break and be back with  more after this remember you can join  the discussion with other viewers on our  reddit forum at david pakman.com slash  read it that's our EDD iti'm on there  every day I'll talk to you there Gamefly  is sort of like Netflix for video games  they have over 8,000 new and classic  games for xbox one Xbox 360 ps4 ps3 Wii  and a bunch of other platforms and you  can play as many games as you want for  one monthly fee and if you find some  games you want to keep you can buy them  for a low used price there's never any  due dates or late fees and our audience  can get a free 30-day trial at Gamefly  offer.com slash TDP s the games are  shipped directly to you with return  envelopes included there's no contracts  and you can rent games through the  mobile app get the free 30-day